          "type": "intro",
          "title": "The Sage of Königsberg",
          "content": "Immanuel Kant (1724-1804) never traveled more than about ten miles from Königsberg, Prussia, in his entire life. Neighbors reportedly set their clocks by his afternoon walk. From this quiet, orderly existence came the most consequential philosophical system since antiquity — one that claimed to finally settle the war between the rationalists and empiricists you studied in the last book.\n\nThe spark was a book, not a battle. Kant later wrote that reading **David Hume** interrupted what he called his \"dogmatic slumber\" and gave his research \"a completely different direction.\" Hume's skepticism about causation didn't just annoy Kant — it convinced him that metaphysics as practiced for two thousand years rested on an unexamined assumption.\n\nIn this lesson, you'll see exactly what that assumption was, and how Kant proposed to overturn it — not by picking a side between reason and experience, but by asking an entirely new kind of question about how minds and objects relate.",
