          "type": "intro",
          "title": "Building the Mind's Architecture",
          "content": "If lesson 1 gave you Kant's revolutionary reversal — objects conform to the mind, not the reverse — this lesson gives you the actual machinery. Kant doesn't just assert that the mind structures experience; he tries to specify, faculty by faculty, exactly what structuring it does and why that structuring is necessary rather than optional.\n\nHe divides cognition into two fundamentally different faculties working together. **Sensibility** passively receives raw sensory input, already shaped by space and time (full treatment in lesson 3). **Understanding** actively organizes that input using built-in concepts Kant calls the **categories**.\n\nThis lesson focuses on the understanding — and on Kant's most ambitious and most difficult argument, the **Transcendental Deduction**, which tries to prove these categories aren't just a convenient list but genuine necessary conditions for having any experience of a coherent, object-filled world at all. Along the way, you'll see Kant's direct attempt to rescue causation itself from Hume's skepticism — the very problem that started this whole project in lesson 1.",
